\section*{Abelianity of special compact Kähler fundamental groups}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
For a special compact Kähler manifold, its entire ordinary fundamental group is claimed to be virtually abelian, including when the manifold has Kodaira dimension zero. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Call a compact complex manifold $X$ special when no line bundle $L$ admits a nonzero map $L\to\Omega_X^p$ with $\kappa(X,L)=p$ for any $1\le p\le\dim X$. For every connected smooth compact Kähler special $X$, OpenAI claims
\[
\exists A\le\pi_1(X):\quad A\text{ abelian},\quad[\pi_1(X):A]<\infty.
\]
Equivalently it claims a finite holomorphic covering, connected and unramified, whose entire discrete fundamental group is abelian. All complex dimensions are included.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 057, 2026-09-23): After maximizing the Albanese first Betti number on finite covers, the proof seeks to make the fiber-group image finite. A minimal infinite quotient is treated either by a semisimple linear representation or by Hilbert-coefficient tensors from low spectrum; both produce positivity that descends, with the actual meridian orders, to a differential sheaf forbidden by specialness.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
